  "why": [
   "Kick and bass take turns. The median reference plays 82% of its bass notes off the beat and only 15% together with the kick, so each owns its moment in the low end: the sub stays clean and the groove bounces between them.",
   "Basslines lean on the root: degrees 1 52%, b7 30%, b2 9%, 6 3%. Staying on 1 and 5 keeps the low end stable under harmony that can drift above it.",
   "The kick is four on the floor in 100% of the references (typical x...x...x...x...): the constant pulse that lets everything else be syncopated.",
   "Clap/snare on 2 and 4 in 100% of them (typical mid-percussion x.xgx.xgx.x.xgx.): the backbeat marks the bar for dancers.",
   "Hats swing: median 49% (middle half 49-50%), timing spread 2 ms. Late off-beat 16ths are what make a straight 4/4 feel played rather than programmed.",
   "Harmony is patient: 1.8 chord changes per 8 bars (median), mostly maj9, m, m9, maj7. Rich but slow chords colour the groove without pulling focus from it.",
   "Breaks work by subtraction: the kick stays in 0% of breaks, the bass in 54%, chords/pads in 77%, vocals in 62%. Taking the pulse away builds the tension the drop releases.",
   "The track unfolds slowly: bass enters at bar 17, chords/stabs at bar 13 (medians). DJs need those drum bars to mix in, and each entry is an event.",
   "Balance: bass -4 dB, chords/stabs -15 dB and vocals -15 dB against the drums (medians). The drums lead; harmony colours from behind."
  ]
